{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Peoples.</b> Of the tribes of Israel." "<b>They will call to the mountain.</b> To Mount Moriah they will gather — every gathering by means of a call is קְרִיאָה, and there <b>they will sacrifice</b> sacrifices <b>of righteousness</b>." "<b>For they will suck the abundance of the seas.</b> Issachar and Zebulun, and they will have leisure to engage in Torah." "<b>And the hidden treasures of the sand.</b> Covered things hidden in the sand — tarit and chilazon and white glass that come forth from the sea and from the sand, and in the portion of Issachar and Zebulun it was as stated in tractate Megillah (6a): ״Zebulun — a people that jeopardized its life״ (Judges 5) — because ״Naphtali — on the heights of the field״ (ibid.), Zebulun would complain about his portion: To one brother You gave fields and vineyards, etc. (Sifrei)." "<b>ושפני.</b> A term of covering, as stated (I Kings 6): ״וַיִּסְפֹּן אֶת הַבַּיִת״, ״וְסָפֻן בָּאֶרֶז״ (Jeremiah 22, 14), and its Targum: ״וּמְטֹלָל בְּכִיּוֹרֵי אַרְזָא״. Another interpretation — <b>Peoples — they will call to the mountain</b>: Through the commerce of Zebulun, merchants of the nations of the world come to his land, and he stands at the border, and they say: Since we have troubled ourselves this far, let us go to Jerusalem and see what is the fear of this nation and what are its deeds. And they see all Israel serving one God and eating one food — for among the nations, the god of this one is not like the god of that one, and the food of this one is not like the food of that one — and they say: There is no nation as worthy as this one, and they convert there, as stated: שָׁם יִזְבְּחוּ זִבְחֵי צֶדֶק (Sifrei)." "<b>For they shall draw of the abundance of the seas.</b> The sea gives Zebulun and Issachar wealth in abundance."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:53.234689129Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}